venu-pacific (26.08.4) trixie; urgency=medium

  * venu-pacific-assistant: stop the maths converter corrupting code. The
    reply was prettified as a single string in query_assistant, before the
    renderer ever split prose from code, so an underscore inside an
    identifier was read as a subscript: compound_interest reached the user
    as "compoundᵢnterest" and csv_reader as "csvᵣeader". Broken code, in a
    coding answer. Replies are now returned verbatim and formatted per
    block, which is the only place that knows which parts are code.
  * A reply consisting only of a code block collapsed to roughly 100px wide
    with every line cut off mid-token, because the scroller had nothing to
    take a natural width from. Code blocks now have a 420px minimum.
  * Saved conversations re-render properly. Scrolling back through history
    showed raw ``` fences and LaTeX, because only freshly-arrived replies
    went through the renderer.

 -- sum abiut <suabiut@gmail.com>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 19:14:22 +1100

venu-pacific (26.08.3) trixie; urgency=medium

  * venu-pacific-assistant: fix two maths rendering faults found in pilot
    testing on real hardware.
  * "$2x + 3$" kept its delimiters. The test for "is this pair of dollar
    signs maths or money?" wanted a backslash, a caret or digit-operator-
    digit, and a variable breaks all three. A short pair with no word of
    three or more letters in it is now treated as an expression, which keeps
    "it costs $5 and then $10 more" as prose.
  * The quadratic formula came out as "frac-b ± √(b² - 4ac)2a". \frac was
    matched with a regex whose arguments could not contain braces, so the
    single most-requested formula in school maths -- whose numerator holds a
    \sqrt{} -- never matched at all. Replaced with a brace-counting parser
    that recurses into its arguments; it now renders (-b ± √(b² - 4ac))/(2a),
    with the denominator bracketed so precedence stays unambiguous.
  * Numbered steps written by the model as one running paragraph are split
    onto separate lines. "is 2x. 2. Differentiate 3x" read as part of the
    equation rather than the start of step two.

 -- sum abiut <suabiut@gmail.com>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 19:00:56 +1100

venu-pacific (26.08.2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * venu-pacific-assistant: render maths and code properly. Pilot testing on
    real hardware found that anything involving calculus or arithmetic came
    out with "the signs all over the place" -- small local models reach for
    LaTeX constantly, and the chat view was a plain GTK label, so \frac{}{},
    x^{2}, \times and $...$ reached the user as literal backslashes and
    braces. Code was worse: no monospace, no colour, no structure at all.
  * LaTeX is now converted to the Unicode the user actually wanted -- proper
    fractions, superscripts, subscripts, Greek letters, roots, and the
    relation symbols. A paired $...$ is treated as a maths delimiter while a
    lone "$48" stays money, so prices are not mangled.
  * Fenced code blocks are lifted out before any of that runs (a backslash in
    a code sample must never be read as LaTeX) and rendered monospace, syntax
    coloured via Pygments, with a language tag and a copy button. Colours are
    drawn from the app's own palette rather than a generic editor theme.
  * SYSTEM_PROMPT now asks for fenced blocks with a language tag and plain
    maths symbols. The renderer is the safety net for when the model ignores
    that, which it often does; the prompt is still the cheaper fix.
  * New dependency: python3-pygments. There is a reduced fallback covering
    comments, strings and numbers if it is ever missing.

 -- sum abiut <suabiut@gmail.com>  Sun, 23 Aug 2026 11:16:16 +1100

venu-pacific (26.08.1) trixie; urgency=medium

  * No functional change to any package. 26.08 was published to the archive
    while the update channel was being brought up, before the ISO had ever
    been built successfully, so that version number is spent: a machine that
    fetched it holds packages identical to these. Bumping rather than
    reusing 26.08 keeps the rule that a version number means one exact set
    of contents, which is the whole basis on which apt decides an upgrade is
    available.
  * The first image that ships to anyone will therefore carry 26.08.1, and
    the archive is republished at the same version so the two agree.

 -- sum abiut <suabiut@gmail.com>  Thu, 20 Aug 2026 18:23:38 +1100

venu-pacific (26.08) trixie; urgency=medium

  * First packaged release. Everything Venu Pacific-specific moves out of
    live-build's includes.chroot and into real Debian packages, so an
    installed system can receive updates through "apt update" instead of
    only through a new 4.7GB image.
  * New venu-pacific-archive-keyring: ships the archive signing key and the
    apt source entry pointing at https://download.venupacific.org/apt.
  * The release version is now defined once, here. /etc/venu-pacific-release,
    the os-release fields, the Help page and the installer title all derive
    from it, replacing the two live-build hooks that used to set it (and
    disagreed with each other about PRETTY_NAME).

 -- sum abiut <suabiut@gmail.com>  Wed, 19 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
